    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Deck skirting is often treated as the final cosmetic flourish of an outdoor project: the finishing touch that hides the frame, pipes and bare soil under the deck. In practice it does much more than improve kerb appeal. Done well, it helps protect the deck's structure, keeps animals out and manages moisture underneath.</p>
<p>Done badly, it can become an expensive problem. Many homeowners see skirting as a simple DIY job, only to face costly repairs a few years later to deal with rot, mould or a weakened frame. Knowing the common pitfalls before you start is the best way to protect your investment and help your garden deck last.</p>

<h2 id="ventilation">1. Ventilation</h2>
<p>The most costly mistake is failing to provide enough ventilation. Many people believe that sealing the space under the deck completely will keep it clean and dry. In reality, the opposite is true. The ground beneath a deck naturally holds moisture, and without airflow that moisture evaporates and becomes trapped against the underside of the frame.</p>
<p>The result is a kind of greenhouse effect. Humidity builds up and speeds up rot, even in pressure-treated timber, and a deck that should have given long service can need major structural repairs much sooner than expected because the joists have gone soft. To avoid this, choose skirting with generous open areas for airflow. If you prefer solid boards, build in decorative metal vents or leave consistent gaps between the boards.</p>

<h2 id="clearance">2. Clearance</h2>
<p>Letting the skirting touch the soil is a recipe for trouble. Even timber rated for ground contact suffers when it is constantly wet. Boards that sit directly on soil or grass act like a sponge, drawing water up into the material, which leads to early warping, peeling paint and fungal growth.</p>
<p>Soil levels also move over time through erosion or frost heave. If the skirting is buried or flush with the ground, that movement can push the panels upwards, buckling the skirting or even putting pressure on the deck's fascia boards. A good rule is to leave a gap of about 25–50 mm (1–2 in) between the bottom of the skirting and the ground. To hide the gap neatly, fill the strip below with decorative gravel or river pebbles, which drain freely while masking the opening.</p>

<h2 id="materials">3. Materials</h2>
<p>Choosing the cheapest material is a classic case of saving money today only to spend more tomorrow. Lightweight plastic lattice is popular because it is inexpensive and easy to fit, but many of these products are not UV-stabilised. After a few seasons the plastic can become brittle, crack and fade, and strong winds can tear the panels from their fixings.</p>
<p>That often means replacing cheap lattice again and again. Better-quality materials such as cellular PVC, composite boards or fibre cement usually make more sense over time. They resist rot, insects and UV damage. They cost noticeably more at the outset, but they typically last far longer, saving you the cost and labour of repeated replacements.</p>

<h2 id="access">4. Access</h2>
<p>Many homeowners fit skirting as a permanent, fixed wall, which is a significant oversight. At some point you, or a professional, will need to get under the deck, whether to retrieve a lost item, inspect the footings, repair a leaking outdoor tap or run cabling for deck lighting.</p>
<p>Without an access panel, you will have to rip out sections of skirting when a problem arises, which means extra repair work and replacement boards that may not match. Build in at least one discreet access door, ideally about 60 × 60 cm (24 × 24 in). Sturdy stainless steel hinges and a simple latch keep the door working and secure for years without spoiling the look of the deck.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Position the access door close to anything you are likely to need to reach, such as an outside tap, a lighting transformer or the footings nearest the house, and make it the full height of the skirting so it is easy to crawl through.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="fasteners">5. Fasteners</h2>
<p>The fixings that hold the skirting in place matter as much as the panels themselves. Interior screws or cheap nails can quickly leave unsightly rust streaks. Worse, as timber or composite expands and contracts with the seasons, weak fixings can snap or pop out, letting the skirting sag or fall off.</p>
<p>Use stainless steel or good-quality coated exterior screws, following the board manufacturer's guidance on the type and spacing. Many people also forget to allow for movement. If you screw a long PVC or composite board down too tightly without pre-drilling oversized holes, it can buckle and warp as it heats up in the sun. The correct fixing technique and the right hardware keep the skirting straight and secure for the long term; check the manufacturer's installation guide for each product.</p>

<h2 id="pests">6. Pests</h2>
<p>The dark, sheltered space under a deck is attractive to wildlife: rats, mice and, depending on where you live, foxes, badgers or, in the US, raccoons and skunks. A common mistake is assuming that the skirting alone will keep them out. Small animals are remarkably good at squeezing through gaps or digging underneath, and once one has moved in, removal and clean-up can be expensive and unpleasant.</p>
<p>To animal-proof your skirting, fit what is sometimes called a trench screen. Fix heavy-duty galvanised wire mesh to the back of the skirting and extend it about 15–30 cm (6–12 in) into the ground, bent outwards in an L shape, so animals digging down meet the mesh and cannot burrow under. Before sealing anything up, make sure nothing is already living under the deck; if you suspect it is, contact a licensed wildlife or pest control professional rather than risk trapping an animal inside. Adding mesh during the initial installation is a small extra job that can save a great deal of cost and hassle later.</p>

<h2 id="aesthetics">7. Aesthetics</h2>
<p>This might sound subjective, but poorly designed skirting can drag down the look of your whole garden and make a well-built deck feel cheap. Skirting that does not suit the style of the house or the colour of the deck looks like an afterthought and breaks the visual flow of the garden, which may not help when it comes to selling.</p>
<p>A frequent mistake is using a different material or colour for the skirting from the deck itself. For a considered, professional finish, match the skirting to the deck's fascia boards or choose something that complements the house's cladding or brickwork. Vertical board-and-batten styles can make a low deck look taller, while horizontal boards give a sleek, modern look. Taking time to coordinate these details means the skirting adds to the appeal of your outdoor space rather than detracting from it.</p>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does every deck need skirting?</h3>
<p>Not necessarily. Low decks, roughly 30 cm (12 in) or less off the ground, often look better without skirting, with planting along the edge instead. For any deck high enough that you can see the frame, skirting is worth considering for looks and to stop animals nesting underneath.</p>
<h3>Can I use pressure-treated timber for my skirting?</h3>
<p>Yes, it is a common and cost-effective choice. Stain or seal it to slow greying and cracking, and keep the gap between the timber and the ground to reduce the risk of rot.</p>
<h3>How much airflow does my deck really need?</h3>
<p>There is no single universal figure. Follow your local building regulations and the decking and skirting manufacturers' guidance, and keep generous gaps or vents along the skirting. If your deck is large, sheltered or the ground underneath is naturally damp, err on the side of more ventilation.</p>
<h3>Is it cheaper to do it myself?</h3>
<p>In terms of upfront labour, usually yes. But if you make the common mistakes of poor ventilation or no animal-proofing, the later cost of repairs and pest removal can easily outweigh what you saved by not hiring a professional installer.</p>
<h3>How do I clean and maintain deck skirting?</h3>
<p>Composite or PVC skirting usually needs no more than an annual wash with mild soapy water. Timber skirting needs re-staining or re-sealing every few years, or as often as the product label recommends, to keep it protected and looking good.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Most skirting problems come down to a handful of decisions made at installation: leave room for air, keep the boards off the soil, choose durable materials and fixings, build in access and mesh, and match the finish to the deck. Get those right and the skirting will protect the deck as well as finish it. For more ideas, read our guides to <a href="/blog/deck-skirting-ideas-small-yards">deck skirting ideas for small yards</a>, <a href="/blog/deck-skirting-cost">deck skirting cost</a> and <a href="/blog/composite-deck-mistakes">composite deck mistakes</a>, or explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service. To plan your outdoor space with a designer, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
